"the probabilities on the right should be interpreted carefully:  for naive bayes, you usually compute:  p(sunny∣yes)= 9 2 	​  p(overcast∣yes)= 9 4 	​  p(rain∣yes)= 9 3 	​   and  p(sunny∣no)= 5 3 	​  p(overcast∣no)= 5 0 	​  p(rain∣no)= 5 2 	​   these are conditional probabilities given the class (yes/no), which is what naive bayes needs.    p(outlook=sunny∣playtennis=yes)=2/9 p(outlook=sunny∣playtennis=no)=3/5  then ✅ everything shown is correct.  the notation written as p(y) and p(no) is a bit misleading; it should really be:  p(sunny∣yes) p(sunny∣no)  and similarly for overcast and rain",neutral
