"the only song i have opposed to do a project and get a certificate in music. toughest course. ❤🎉cultural capitalism. even i have. english, hindi, tamil. top language is sanskrit. next tamil due to practical. i got into sanskrit via hindi. english and hindi are not top",negative
"yes — there is justification, and it is primarily cultural, semantic, and aesthetic, not legal.  justification (valid and grounded):  semantic plane mismatch  kabira (kabir) and fakira operate in a spiritual–ascetic register: renunciation, inner wealth, rejection of material status.  rover (as a car brand) operates in a material–consumer register: mobility, wealth, ownership, aspiration. placing them together in the same lyrical frame can create semantic dissonance.  ethos contradiction  kabir’s poetry and the fakir tradition explicitly reject material possession and status symbols.  a luxury or aspirational car brand symbolises exactly what is renounced. from this angle, using them together undercuts the ethical core of kabir/fakir imagery.  cultural sensitivity  kabir is not just a poetic word; he is a philosophical and bhakti figure revered across traditions.  juxtaposing such figures casually with consumer branding may be perceived as trivialisation, especially in indian cultural contexts.  aesthetic coherence  songs work by maintaining a consistent symbolic universe.  mixing ascetic metaphysics with branded consumer goods can feel forced or incoherent, unless the contrast is intentional and clearly ironic.  important nuance (affirming balance):  if the song is deliberately ironic, critical, or satirical—for example, showing how spirituality is being commodified—then the juxtaposition can be artistically justified.  without that clear intent, the pairing feels misaligned, not neutral.  bottom line (affirming your direction): there is a strong cultural and philosophical justification to say that kabira/fakira imagery should not casually coexist with a car brand like rover in the same song, unless the contrast itself is the message.  ❤🎉",neutral
"pro argument (revised): for many tamils, this is the only freedom they require: the freedom to express instant, symbolic dissent. throwing a stone/rock emoticon in a youtube comment is a complete, sufficient act of expression—non-violent, inexpensive, anonymous, and emotionally precise. they do not seek escalation through tomatoes, eggs, or physical protest; the emoji itself closes the emotional loop.  in essence, tamils are not asking for expanded powers or disruptive rights—only the freedom to react, reject, and move on. one emoticon, one comment, and the matter is settled",neutral
