"when everything you say a bs  the trump administration's policy shifts have negatively impacted low-income children by weakening financial safety nets, reducing food security, and dismantling established public health protections [american progress]. a central driver of this impact is the one big beautiful bill act (obbba), which enacted deep funding cuts to medicaid and chip, threatening health insurance coverage for millions of vulnerable youth [american progress]. financial strain on families has been further compounded by the administration's rejection of a fully refundable child tax credit, a decision that excludes the poorest families from crucial economic relief and keeps child poverty rates elevated near 13.4% [american progress]. simultaneously, the administration reduced food security by cutting billions in local food purchasing grants, scaling back wic benefits for fruits and vegetables, and tightening eligibility rules for snap [the center on budget and policy priorities].in early childhood development, protections have been scaled back through the deregulation of the federal head start program [the center on budget and policy priorities]. by removing strict federal performance standards on safety protocols and staff-to-student ratios, the administration has transferred oversight to individual states, a move that critics warn allows for lower care standards and makes it easier for programs to exclude children with disabilities [the center on budget and policy priorities]. finally, sweeping changes to public health guidelines have disrupted routine pediatric care [the center on budget and policy priorities]. an executive order recommending a reduction in routine childhood vaccines from 17 to 11, alongside the unbundling of combined shots, has placed a heavier medical and financial burden on low-income parents while increasing the risk of preventable disease outbreaks [the center on budget and policy priorities]. these domestic rollbacks, paired with the 