"the pellet weighs much more than the bb, so the amount of foot pounds of energy going into the bottle by the pellet is much higher than the bb. that umarex that shot the pellet can also fire bb's. and if you shot them both from that gun, the pellet would have caused more damage. fpe= weight (in grains) x velocity² (in fps) ÷ 450240 (to convert the result to foot pounds). so take the weight of the object and the speed its fired, and you can find out how many foot pounds of energy are impacting the target. a larger projectile will always hit harder than the smaller one going the same speed. but also, a larger pellet by just a few grains will hit with higher foot pounds than a lighter pellet or bb traveling a biy faster. the only way to have the lighter/faster projectile hit with the same force is for it to go much, much faster than the heavier object. think about it: which hits harder? a person on motorcycle going 60 mph, or a train going 10mph? the gun on a bike who runs into the wall of a building is going to get stopped by the wall. but the train going 10mph would run right through the building, and the the next building without even slowing down very much. that because the train weighs hundreds of thousands of pounds and the bike weigh a few hundred. the point is, even though the bb may travel a little faster in fps, its much lighter than the pellets, so the pellet hits with more energy becauee it weighs more",neutral
