"is it possible that the wealth transfer you mention between the old oligarchs and the new oligarchs/public is one of the causes of the war starting or continuing?  my thought is that the old oligarchs would have split loyalties which could make for problems. you can't get rid of them violently without sparking unrest among the rest. but you can slowly bleed them dry since it gives no spike in the incentive to rise up, so you have the same problem as protesters not knowing how many others will show up.   the new oligarchs though would have a much less split loyalty since they only have their wealth because of putin and their wealth will continue to grow as long as the war continues. so, they give putin a new stable foundation that he may be able to trust a lot more than the old oligarchs.   follow up: would this be part of the explaination for why the war continues? because the old oligarchs are still experiencing losses while the new oligarchs are experiencing gains, which can only continue with the war ongoing? would it be possible for the wealth transfer to continue at a similar or higher rate without the war? or does war, despite its costs, represent an apex",negative
"i think it's important to realign the lens a bit. 1. by design, oligarchs are non-political. also by design, they are viewed negatively by the public. putin saw to this in the first years of his tenure (a la absconders of state assets for fire sale prices) and there's little doubt those feeling have changed all that much. 2. while media may portray the ""pro war"" camp as homogenous, it's anything but. you have actors who hold political power in offices across government and then you have the siloviki, a group of trusted men that make up putin's inner circle. for simplicity, i'll use the terms bureaucrats and siloviki.  if history is a guide (which it very well might not be), the only time russia has a somewhat fair election is when there is no incumbent. if we assume this premise, then the next president will need to be popular.   still no conclusion, but i think this framing is integral to addressing the question. but if we see a siloviki henchman making a political appearance (ie, sechin addressing the nation or running for a council seat), i think we will have our answer. the one thing we know for sure is that the winner must act fast when the time is right",neutral
