"hi, as a russian i think you might not quite understand what factions there even are in russian internal politics. right now, truly, there's basically nobody apart from the president himself who supports the war. even the militarists at the very bottom are not very keen on continuing, since the war is just way, way too costly. the situation has gone to the point that nobody likes or benefits from the war much, but they still keep silent because the power has been collecting and concentrating really hard in hands of, basically, the president's personal security (the federal guard service fso, and the 2nd service of the fsb). even governors and said oligarchs have lost their power, because they've voluntarily given it up before the war for profit  therefore, while it is not certain, it is a lot more likely that the groups in the government, regardless of their actual standing and opinion, would coalesce on somehow stopping the war.  but what's even more likely is the state of anocracy / fragile state, since the president's death would yoink the the nail holding everyone pinned under the rule of the security forces - if the heads of fsb services don't catch the power as it's falling. because what the war did - it has allowed those oligarchs, governors and even individual factories to arm themselves, and to create a plethora of privateer companies",negative
