"0:45 i'm not an engineer, but as a programmer, this would be my tipoff that this was fake. that rotation. why? it's not the idiocy of trying to make a *robot* dizzy by spinning it, no that's only the second thing i would think of, even as i type this, the rest was written first: that ""robot"" starts spinning itself in circles at the slightest tug, and when let go of, continues to spin itself around to complete the rotation. 1. a real robot would be programmed to need much more of a pull because, if anything, you'd want it to ignore or counter-balance itself against the strength of a stiff breeze. 2. a real robot wouldn't continue it's own rotation unnecessarily. if it was programmed to keep the targets in sight, it would be programmed to take the shortest rotation back. not amuse the handler by spinning itself around just to prove that it can't become dizzy. these unnecessary actions would not be programmed in unless they *wanted* to make it look like a human in a robot costume. now for some more funny debunks: the sound of it hitting the barrel it tripped over came before it even hit the barrel, let alone that sound being very hollow for a plastic barrel that is lying on the ground which would not let it eccho that much. they then shove the robot to the ground *after* it already took a shot, which could have been an accident, but real humans wouldn't risk that even with a robot they trust. it reels from being hit over the head, but doesn't show any physical reactions to the mass hitting it, exactly like people being hit over the head in the movies, often with obviously fake, delayed reactions. then the robot gets triggered by the guy shooting as its feet.. sure. but when standing there all but motionless, gun pointed to the floor, the robot responds like it's on the *highest* alert mode, like the gunshots didn't faze it, but that stare down made it leak its pants, lol. *then* the robot, which has been shown to be an excellent shot, gets triggered and shoots not one but *multiple* shots straight ahead without even appearing to aim. just.. hilarious. not to mention it's little tantrum slamming the gun on the table and flipping it. to any non-programmers out there, this behaviour has to be intentionally built in for it to ever show. why would *anyone* implement this kind of dangerous and unpredictable behaviour? no. a robot will not get frustrated after being beat over the head and spat on. irl, it would ignore all of these things completely. then the plastic tape it goes underneath? why? to protect the structural integrity of a flimsy tape meant to keep *humans* out? no, a robot would walk through that, because we're not going to send in a killer robot if we still want the rest of the place still in tact to the point that we would want it to avoid warning tape! trying to decide which of the targets is the dummy? do you *want* the robot to only shoot target dummies or real criminals? because this robot apparently prefers to only shoot dummies. also, if we wanted it to shoot either, it wouldn't hesitate. we would give it infrared camera's so it could see easily which one is a cold target dummy and which is a warm blooded human. that push afterwards served no purpose but for comedy",neutral
