"what stood out to me is how much of this conversation is really about incentives and infrastructure rather than raw capability. lambert and raschka keep circling back to the point that who ""wins"" ai depends more on compute budgets and business models than on any single breakthrough one lab has that others don't. that's a quieter, less dramatic story than the one usually told, where everything hinges on some secret architectural edge. it reframes the ai race less as a story about genius and more as a story about logistics, which is honestly a little unsettling, since it means the trajectory of something this consequential is being steered by spreadsheets as much as by ideas",neutral
