"america’s bond market: a warning signal for the world  the us treasury’s decision to increase buybacks of long-duration government bonds, after the 30-year treasury yield touched around 5.3%, is more than routine debt management. it does not mean america has run out of buyers. rather, investors are demanding higher returns for holding 20–30 year us debt because of huge fiscal deficits, rising debt-service costs, inflation uncertainty and longer-term interest-rate risk. the treasury is effectively trying to support the long end of the bond market while relying relatively more on short-term borrowing. this can reduce borrowing costs temporarily, but it also increases refinancing risk if short-term rates remain high. the market reaction is significant: bond yields eased, the dollar weakened and gold strengthened. if this trend persists, it could signal a gradual erosion of the traditional “us exceptionalism” premium—not a collapse of the dollar, but greater diversification toward gold and other assets. high crude prices make the situation more difficult. oil-driven inflation + high debt + slowing growth creates a potential stagflationary environment, limiting the fed’s freedom to cut rates aggressively.  what does it mean for india? lower us yields could benefit indian equities, bonds and capital flows. but high crude prices could widen india’s import bill, pressure the rupee and revive inflation. for indian investors, gold gains strategic importance as a hedge against currency, inflation and global fiscal risks. the two indicators worth watching most closely are us 30-year treasury yields and crude oil prices. their direction could shape the global investment environment over the next 6–12 months",negative
