"the expectations point deserves even more attention. markets don’t simply price good news or bad news—they price **better or worse than what was already expected**.  that’s the part that breaks most people’s intuition. a stock can rally on objectively bad news if the news is simply less bad than investors feared. likewise, great news can send a stock lower if expectations were even higher.  by the time you read the headline, much of the information may already be reflected in the price. that’s why trading the headline instead of the surprise relative to expectations often means retail investors are arriving late to the move",neutral
