"there is just one small flaw in his example which used to be true but is no longer.  if a company buys back its stock, it shrinks its market cap.  and a smaller market cap means less investment by passive investors.  for instance, if apple bought back half its shares, it would no longer see the outstanding movements in its stock like it did before the buyback. so what do insiders do?  they issue more shares to themselves and then they go on a regular selling cycle.  they don't have to work any harder, they just reap the benefits at the expense of other shareholders",neutral
