"the expectations point deserves even more weight than he gives it. markets don't price good or bad—they price better or worse than already expected. this is the piece that breaks most people's intuition, because it means stocks can rip higher on objectively bad news that simply came in less bad than feared. the news you're reading is usually already in the price by the time you read it. trading the headline instead of the surprise is how retail investors consistently arrive late.  where i'd add nuance is the mechanical stuff—passive flows, buybacks, 0dte gamma. all three are real and he explains them well, but there's a slight over-attribution to plumbing here. these are amplifiers, not the engine. passive flows and gamma squeezes exaggerate moves in both directions, and buybacks flatter eps, but none of it levitates prices indefinitely without underlying earnings to stand on—the same mechanics that ramp a market also accelerate it downward when flows reverse. worth keeping the causation ordered: fundamentals set the direction, structure sets the speed and the overshoot",neutral
