"interesting video,. i don't see your videos that often as the algorithm doesn't send them my way. also i suppose they don't fit my investing profile. i am a retired investment analyst, don't need side hustles etc. however i was walking the dog and gave it a listen. the more financial education offered out there the better and the more people realise they can invest the better as well. i did have a couple of observations about what you said. the uk economy against all odds is doing better than most of the g7, not exactly cause for champagne and what come next might not be so good. the ftse 100 is also very resources and financials biased and they are largely beneficiaries or insulated from current geopolitical tensions and inflation. over in the us which is about 45% of global mkt value the last two earnings seasons have been pretty strong, most of the big companies have reported excellent earnings, palantir's being probably the best ever seen for a $300bn company. the financials also reported mostly strong results. most beat positively rather than simply not being as bad as feared. there has also been a lot of stock and sector rotation this year so the market high is by no means due to the mag 7, meta and tesla are down, microsoft flat the other 4 up between 9% and 16%. the strength is more broad based than the doom mongers would have us believe. finally the ai trade is changing. demand for ai compute is still growing fast but it is also becoming a commodity.however, that fact does not diminish the demand for the ""picks and shovels"" which is both huge and supply constrained. whatever triggers the next crash when it eventually comes probably won't be valuations and certainly not valuations alone. more likely it will be something in the usually bigger  debt market that causes panic to set in",neutral
