if warsh has an issue with this then he has one guy to blame: jpow.  if jpow wouldn't have loaded up on mortgage backed securities back in 2021/2022 then the fed wouldn't be offloading those securities right now and long term rates would be lower.  and if jpow (and warsh) would have actually raised the short term rates to keep inflation under control then you wouldn't have as much of a spread between the durations.  if you're going to give the treasury an opportunity to borrow at artificially low short term rates then you can't blame them for taking it.  should they be doing this when the fed's profits (back when they had them) all go to the treasury?  maybe not.  but the fed shouldn't be creating 2% mortgages when the cost of that is ultimately bourn by the treasury and the us taxpayers.    the thing about off-the-run buybacks doesn't really matter though.  maybe there's a billion dollars worth of 10y bonds paying 2% with five years to maturity.  you can buy them for roughly $880m using short term money you borrowed at 3.6%.  doing that causes your interest expense goes up but the total debt outstanding goes down by $120m.  that buyback isn't what saves you money.  what saves you money is the short term rates being lower than the current ten year rates,negative
