"if i didn’t know better i’d swear to god that someone had secretly videotaped this same conversation between me and my father. my father was a member of the greatest generation. but the one thing men from this generation failed at was bothering to understand and be an integral part of their children’s lives. and this pertained to the sons. the daughters fared much better because they had their mothers who were more in tune with relating to their daughters.  my dad was a war hero, but i think what it cost him was something that if he had to do things over again he might have thought twice about wanting a direct combat role. i think that during world war two, there was immense pressure on young men to be a part of the actual fighting and that resulted in those same young men who survived coming back to society screwed up. the level of knowledge regarding ptsd back then was minuscule to what it is today. as a b17 pilot who flew 35 combat missions over nazi germany, my father saw things that humans were not meant to see. i can only imagine the horrors he witnessed and how and why he had a tough time relating to his sons. as long as he clothed, housed and fed us, he felt he did his duty. but i think he failed to prepare us for the battle of simply living and that duty he deferred to our teachers, friends, employers and total strangers. in other words, people who really didn’t care one way or the other what happened to us. but, as my mother said, “your father did his best.”  what else could any of us expect her to say",neutral
