"the idea of an ""islamic golden age"" as a purely islamic-driven scientific revolution is overstated. the abbasid caliphate in baghdad prospered primarily due to its strategic location on major trade routes, which brought immense wealth and facilitated the flow of ideas from diverse civilizations. this economic boom funded the famous translation movement, where scholars—many of them non-muslims, including christians, jews, and persians—translated ancient greek, indian, persian, and syriac texts into arabic. these works formed the foundation for later advancements. key mathematical innovations often credited broadly to this era actually originated elsewhere:  the concept of zero as a placeholder and the decimal positional system were developed by indian mathematicians (e.g., aryabhata and brahmagupta) centuries earlier. the pythagorean theorem appears in the indian sulba sutras (dating to around 800–500 bce), predating pythagoras, and was also known to the babylonians. al-khwarizmi played a crucial role in synthesizing and popularizing these ideas—he drew heavily on indian sources for numerals and arithmetic, while his seminal work on solving equations established algebra as a distinct discipline (the word ""algebra"" derives from his book al-jabr).  in essence, the abbasid era excelled at preserving, translating, and building upon pre-existing knowledge from older civilizations, rather than inventing it from scratch under islamic inspiration alone. this cosmopolitan exchange, enabled by trade and tolerant patronage, was the true driver of progress—not a unique religious impetus",neutral
