"algebra was founded in the ancient middle east, particularly in mesopotamia, where early civilizations such as the babylonians (circa 2000 bce) developed techniques for solving equations, including quadratic and linear equations. however, algebra as we know it today was formalized much later by islamic scholars.  the term “algebra” comes from the arabic word “al-jabr,” which means “reunion of broken parts.” it was popularized by the persian mathematician al-khwarizmi (c. 780–850 ce) in his book “kitab al-mukhtasar fi hisab al-jabr wal-muqabala”. this book laid the foundations for systematic solving of linear and quadratic equations and gave the field its name.  so, while algebra’s roots go back to ancient babylon, its formalization and systematization were solidified in the islamic golden age",neutral
